{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c021", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Reimbursable \"Out-of-Pocket Expenses\" are limited to those \"Incurred routinely,\" a term that draws no line between routine and extraordinary outlays. The defined term \"Expenses\" is then used inconsistently with the flat six-percent charge in subparagraph (a).", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c022", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This subparagraph charges a flat 6% of professional fees for the listed categories, which cannot be squared with section 4.2's premise that Xenophon is \"entitled to relmbursement for Out-of-Pocket Expenses Incurred,\" nor with section 5.1(a), which calls the same 6% a \"professlonal fee\" while parenthetically labelling it \"Out-of-Pocket Expenses.\" Whether the 6% is in lieu of, or additional to, actual costs in those categories is unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c023", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The parenthetical defining \"non-local travel\" as \"(I.e., transportation, lodging, meals and related expenses)\" is circular — meals are both the head term and part of its own definition — and \"non-local\" is nowhere delimited against the \"local transportation charges\" covered by the 6% fee in subparagraph (a). Which bucket a given trip falls into is therefore contestable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c024", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The Third-party Expenses list is opened by \"without limitation\" and closed by \"other vendor and/or subcontractor charges, or others,\" a catch-all with no ascertainable boundary against the enumerated specifics. Almost any cost Xenophon incurs could be pushed into this category.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c025", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This paragraph adds an \"Industry standard mark up of 17,65%\" to all third-party expenses, while section 4.2(c) obliges the Client to pay only \"the cost of Third-party Expenses upon recelpt.\" Cost and cost-plus-markup cannot both be the measure of the same charge.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c026", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Section 5.1's promise of \"monthly invoices detalllng Its fees and expenses\" sits against subparagraph (b), under which travel and third-party expenses are invoiced \"after costs are Incurred\" rather than monthly. Whether expenses must appear on the monthly invoice or may be billed ad hoc is unclear from the two read together.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.17}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c027", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause calls the 6% charge a \"professlonal fee\" and then parenthetically labels it \"Out-of-Pocket Expenses,\" two mutually inconsistent characterisations of the same item that also affect whether the 6% is itself part of the base on which it is computed. The mislabeling is on the face of this provision.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24},
    {"issue": "This subparagraph defers the \"detalled summary of billable time\" to \"the following month,\" whereas section 4.1 requires Xenophon to \"supply full detail to the cliant along with them monthly Involces.\" The two timing requirements for the same backup cannot both be satisfied.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c028", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The first Expense Invoice is set for \"on or about December 1, 2014\" even though the Period of Performance begins October 20, 2014 and expenses are to be invoiced \"after costs are Incurred,\" leaving October and November expense billing unaccounted for. The date and the incurred-cost trigger pull against each other.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c029", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"[A]n interest charge equal to Its own borrowing rate\" ties the rate to a figure known only to Xenophon and nowhere disclosed, defined or capped. Neither the rate nor the compounding period can be determined from the Agreement.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c030", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Section 6.1 lets the Client \"stop any schedules or work In progress\" immediately, which is difficult to square with section 2.2's requirement of 60 days' written notice to terminate. Whether a total stop-work order is a permitted modification or a disguised termination triggering the notice period is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c031", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The Client's indemnity here is expressly carved back for losses arising from \"Xenophon's negligent acts or omissions,\" whereas section 6.1(iii) requires the Client to indemnify Xenophon for all third-party claims flowing from carrying out Client instructions with no negligence exception. The two indemnities overlap and set different limits on the same risk.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c032", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"[B]est efforts to safeguard the confidentiality\" of \"all proprietary and other confidential Information\" defines neither the standard of effort nor what qualifies as confidential, and no marking or designation procedure is provided. The duty runs only against Xenophon, leaving the Client unrestricted.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c033", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The provision imposes a duty to \"endeavor, to the best of Its ability, to guard agalnst any loss\" and in the same sentence disclaims all responsibility \"for any such fallure,\" so it is unclear whether any enforceable obligation survives. The two halves of the sentence point in opposite directions.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.26}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c034", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"[T]he prevailing party\" is undefined, with no rule for partial success or for a dispute resolved short of judgment, and the fee right is limited to disputes \"concerning any alleged breach\" rather than all disputes under the Agreement. Claims in quantum meruit or for declaratory relief may fall outside the clause.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c035", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The bar on delegating \"any of Its dutles or obligations\" without written consent sits against section 4.2(c) and section 7.3, which assume Xenophon will engage subcontractors and suppliers to perform the Services. Whether routine subcontracting is a prohibited delegation is not resolved by either provision.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c036", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause says that if a provision is illegal or invalid, \"sald legality or invalidity shall not affect the remaining provisions\" — the word should be \"illegality,\" and as written the sentence preserves the wrong condition. The mechanical error is on the face of the severability language.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.13}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c037", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The first sentence vests the Client with ownership of \"any materlals developed or created on behalf of the Client\" as works made for hire, while the third sentence gives Xenophon copyright in \"any drafts or other non-final work product\" and in the methodologies used. The line between Client-owned Deliverables and Xenophon-owned drafts and methodologies is not drawn.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c038", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause permits change \"only by a written agreement signed by the Parties,\" yet section 1.1's \"Other Tasks\" and sections 1.2, 4.2 and 6.1 all contemplate work, expenses and instructions authorised by informal \"client approval\" or agreement. Whether such approvals are effective without a signed writing is unresolved between these provisions.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c039", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause deems notice delivered by hand, express courier or certified mail but says nothing about email or ordinary mail, and gives no attention line for the Client, so the validity of a notice sent by other means — including a section 2.2 termination notice — is uncertain. \"[A]nother national recognized express delivery service\" is likewise undefined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14}
  ]}
]}

